---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import ArticleOffer from '../../components/ArticleOffer.astro';

const title = 'pgvector HNSW Index Build Slow? Memory, Parallel Workers and /dev/shm';
const description = 'Lab-tested on pgvector 0.8.7 and PostgreSQL 18: a 100,000-row HNSW build took 9 minutes at the default maintenance_work_mem and under 2 at 1GB. Then why parallel workers never started for 768-dimension vectors, and the Docker shared-memory error behind failed builds.';

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'Why is my pgvector HNSW index build so slow?',
    a: 'Usually because the graph does not fit in maintenance_work_mem. pgvector prints "hnsw graph no longer fits into maintenance_work_mem after N tuples" and continues much more slowly. In a test with 100,000 768-dimension vectors, the build took 9 minutes 4 seconds at the 64MB default and between 1 minute 33 seconds and 2 minutes 2 seconds at 1GB.'
  },
  {
    q: 'How much maintenance_work_mem does an HNSW build need?',
    a: 'Enough to hold the whole graph, which is roughly the size of the finished index. In the test, the index was 391 MB and a 1GB setting kept the build in memory. Set it per session for the build, and leave headroom for the rest of the server; parallel builds also allocate that amount as shared memory.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Why does my HNSW build not use parallel workers?',
    a: 'PostgreSQL decides the number of workers from the size of the table heap. Vectors of more than about 500 dimensions are stored out of line in TOAST, so the heap stays small. In the test, the heap was 5.9 MB against 391 MB of TOAST, below the 8MB min_parallel_table_scan_size, so no workers started. Setting the table reloption parallel_workers made them start.'
  },
  {
    q: 'What does "could not resize shared memory segment ... No space left on device" mean during an HNSW build?',
    a: 'The parallel build tried to allocate maintenance_work_mem as a POSIX shared memory segment in /dev/shm, and /dev/shm was too small. Docker gives containers 64MB by default. Start the container with --shm-size at least as large as maintenance_work_mem, or mount a larger memory-backed volume at /dev/shm in Kubernetes.'
  },
  {
    q: 'Do more parallel workers always make HNSW builds faster?',
    a: 'No. In the test, two workers cut the build from about 1 minute 45 seconds to 53 seconds, while four and eight workers took 70 and 74 seconds. Workers insert into one shared graph and contend for it. Measure two or three settings on a copy of your data before picking one.'
  }
];
---
