  <h2 id="setup">The test setup</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>pgvector/pgvector:0.8.7-pg18</code> image: PostgreSQL 18.6, pgvector 0.8.7, on a laptop with 10 CPU cores and 8 GB of memory available to Docker.</li>
    <li>One table, <code>items(id bigint, embedding vector(768))</code>, 100,000 rows of random values. 768 dimensions is a common size for sentence-embedding models.</li>
    <li>Index: <code>CREATE INDEX … USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops)</code> with the defaults <code>m = 16</code> and <code>ef_construction = 64</code>. The finished index was 391 MB.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>Random vectors are a harder case for graph construction than real embeddings, which cluster. Your absolute times will differ. The ratios between settings are what carry over.</p>

  <h2 id="memory">maintenance_work_mem: the 5x setting</h2>
  <p>At the default <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> of 64MB, pgvector stopped building in memory after about 18,000 of the 100,000 rows:</p>
<pre><code>{`NOTICE:  hnsw graph no longer fits into maintenance_work_mem after 17697 tuples
DETAIL:  Building will take significantly more time.
HINT:  Increase maintenance_work_mem to speed up builds.`}</code></pre>
  <p>From that point it inserts the remaining rows into the on-disk index one at a time, which is much slower than building in memory. Serial build times:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col"><code>maintenance_work_mem</code></th><th scope="col">Build time</th><th scope="col">Graph in memory?</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>64MB (default)</td><td>9 min 4 s; 11 min 49 s in a run that requested 2 workers, which did not start</td><td>Only the first 17,697 rows</td></tr>
      <tr><td>1GB</td><td>1 min 33 s to 2 min 2 s across 9 runs</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>That is a 5x difference from one setting, and it grows with the table: the in-memory part stays the same size while the slow part grows. Set it for the session that runs the build, not globally:</p>
<pre><code>{`SET maintenance_work_mem = '1GB';   -- more than the expected index size
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY items_embedding_hnsw
  ON items USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);
RESET maintenance_work_mem;`}</code></pre>
  <p>To size it, estimate the index from a build on a sample, or use the existing index size if you are rebuilding. The graph needs roughly the finished index size. Do not set it so high that the build pushes the rest of the server into swap or the out-of-memory killer. On RDS or Aurora, check the instance class memory and what the instance already uses, and remember that a parallel build also allocates that amount as shared memory, below.</p>

  <h2 id="workers">Why parallel workers did not start</h2>
  <p>pgvector supports parallel HNSW builds, and <code>max_parallel_maintenance_workers</code> defaults to 2. In the first round of tests, though, 2, 4 and 8 workers made no difference: 1 minute 46 seconds to 2 minutes 1 second, the same as serial. Sampling <code>pg_stat_activity</code> during the builds showed why:</p>
<pre><code>{`=== shm=4g maintenance_work_mem=1GB workers=4
sample 1: workers=0 cpu=102.19%
sample 2: workers=0 cpu=99.71%
sample 3: workers=0 cpu=100.08%`}</code></pre>
  <p>No workers, one core busy. PostgreSQL decides how many workers an index build gets from the size of the table's <em>heap</em>, using <code>min_parallel_table_scan_size</code> (8MB by default) as the threshold. The <code>vector</code> type uses <code>EXTERNAL</code> storage, and a 768-dimension vector takes about 3 KB, above the roughly 2 KB at which PostgreSQL moves values out of line. So the vectors live in the TOAST table and the heap holds little more than pointers:</p>
<pre><code>{`SELECT pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size('items')) AS heap,
       pg_relation_size('items') / current_setting('block_size')::int AS heap_pages,
       pg_size_pretty(pg_relation_size(reltoastrelid)) AS toast,
       current_setting('min_parallel_table_scan_size') AS min_parallel_table_scan_size
FROM pg_class WHERE oid = 'items'::regclass;

  heap   | heap_pages | toast  | min_parallel_table_scan_size
---------+------------+--------+------------------------------
 5888 kB |        736 | 391 MB | 8MB`}</code></pre>
  <p>A 5.9 MB heap is below the threshold, so the planner assigned zero workers. Raising <code>max_parallel_maintenance_workers</code> changes nothing in that case. The same applies to most embeddings from 512 dimensions up, including 1,536- and 3,072-dimension models. A <code>halfvec(768)</code> column, at about 1.5 KB per value, would usually stay in the heap.</p>
  <p>The table storage parameter <code>parallel_workers</code> overrides the size-based calculation. With it set, the workers started:</p>
  <div class="table-wrap"><table>
    <thead><tr><th scope="col">Workers (<code>parallel_workers</code> and <code>max_parallel_maintenance_workers</code>)</th><th scope="col">Workers seen</th><th scope="col">Container CPU</th><th scope="col">Build time</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>0 (no reloption)</td><td>0</td><td>~100%</td><td>1 min 33 s – 2 min 2 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td>2</td><td>2</td><td>302%</td><td>53 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td>4</td><td>4</td><td>503%</td><td>1 min 10 s</td></tr>
      <tr><td>8</td><td>8</td><td>970%</td><td>1 min 14 s</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table></div>
  <p>Two workers halved the build. Four and eight used more CPU and were slower than two: the leader and workers insert into one shared graph and contend for it. Your best number depends on cores, dimensions and data, so time two or three settings on a copy. The procedure that worked:</p>
<pre><code>{`ALTER TABLE items SET (parallel_workers = 2);
SET maintenance_work_mem = '1GB';
SET max_parallel_maintenance_workers = 2;
CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY items_embedding_hnsw
  ON items USING hnsw (embedding vector_cosine_ops);
ALTER TABLE items RESET (parallel_workers);`}</code></pre>
  <p>Reset the option afterwards. <code>parallel_workers</code> also sets the number of workers for parallel queries that scan the table, so leaving it set changes query plans, not just index builds. The workers come from <code>max_parallel_workers</code> and <code>max_worker_processes</code>, both 8 by default; if those are already in use by parallel queries, the build gets fewer workers than it asked for.</p>

  <ArticleOffer offer="pgvector" title="Rebuilding vector indexes for a new model, and the build window keeps growing?" text="I measure build time, memory and recall for your dimensions and data on a copy, pick m, ef_construction, memory and worker settings from the results, and write the build and cutover procedure for production." />

  <h2 id="shm">The /dev/shm error in Docker and Kubernetes</h2>
  <p>A parallel build places the graph in dynamic shared memory, sized by <code>maintenance_work_mem</code>. On Linux, PostgreSQL's default <code>dynamic_shared_memory_type</code> is <code>posix</code>, which allocates from <code>/dev/shm</code>. Docker gives a container 64MB there unless told otherwise. With workers running and <code>maintenance_work_mem = 1GB</code>, the build failed at once:</p>
<pre><code>{`ERROR:  could not resize shared memory segment "/PostgreSQL.323454400" to 1070632480 bytes: No space left on device`}</code></pre>
  <p>1,070,632,480 bytes is just under 1 GiB: the graph's share of the 1GB <code>maintenance_work_mem</code>, requested as one segment. The serial builds never hit this, which is why it surfaces only when someone adds workers, or when a table grows enough for the planner to start them on its own. The fix is to give <code>/dev/shm</code> more room than <code>maintenance_work_mem</code>:</p>
<pre><code>{`# Docker
docker run --shm-size=2g ... pgvector/pgvector:0.8.7-pg18

# docker compose
services:
  db:
    image: pgvector/pgvector:0.8.7-pg18
    shm_size: 2gb

# Kubernetes: memory-backed volume at /dev/shm (counts toward the pod's memory)
volumes:
  - name: dshm
    emptyDir:
      medium: Memory
      sizeLimit: 2Gi
# container.volumeMounts: - name: dshm, mountPath: /dev/shm`}</code></pre>
  <p>The pgvector README gives the same advice for Docker: keep <code>--shm-size</code> at least as large as <code>maintenance_work_mem</code>. If an operator runs your PostgreSQL pods, check how it sizes <code>/dev/shm</code> before raising <code>maintenance_work_mem</code> on a containerized server. Managed services such as RDS and Aurora handle shared memory themselves.</p>

  <h2 id="progress">Watch a build in progress</h2>
  <p>HNSW builds report through the standard index progress view. pgvector uses two phases, <code>initializing</code> and <code>loading tuples</code>; the view shows the second as <code>building index: loading tuples</code>:</p>
<pre><code>{`SELECT p.pid, p.phase, p.tuples_done,
       c.reltuples::bigint AS estimated_rows,
       round(100.0 * p.tuples_done / nullif(c.reltuples, 0), 1) AS pct,
       now() - a.query_start AS elapsed
FROM pg_stat_progress_create_index AS p
JOIN pg_stat_activity AS a USING (pid)
JOIN pg_class AS c ON c.oid = p.relid;`}</code></pre>
  <p>In the lab, during the loading phase <code>tuples_total</code> was 0 while the block counters already read 736 of 736, because they count the small heap. <code>tuples_done</code> was the useful counter, so the query above compares it with the planner's row estimate. A build that has printed the "no longer fits" notice slows down after that point, so do not extrapolate the finish time from the first minutes. If a concurrent build is waiting rather than working, see <a href="/insights/postgresql-create-index-concurrently-stuck">CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY stuck?</a></p>

  <h2 id="production">Build procedure for production</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Rehearse on a copy</strong> with the real vectors. Record the index size, the build time and whether the notice appears.</li>
    <li><strong>Set memory for the session</strong> above the rehearsed index size, within what the server can spare.</li>
    <li><strong>Check worker planning</strong> with the heap and TOAST query. If the heap is smaller than <code>min_parallel_table_scan_size</code>, set <code>parallel_workers</code> for the build and reset it afterwards. In containers, check <code>/dev/shm</code> first.</li>
    <li><strong>Build with <code>CONCURRENTLY</code></strong> so writes continue. It takes longer and waits for running transactions; schedule it outside the peak.</li>
    <li><strong>Validate</strong>: check <code>indisvalid</code>, then measure recall and latency against exact search for representative queries, including filtered ones. A fast build with poor recall is not a result. See <a href="/insights/pgvector-hnsw-vs-ivfflat">HNSW vs IVFFlat</a> and <a href="/insights/pgvector-filtered-search-missing-results">filtered search returning too few rows</a>.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>If you are on pgvector 0.8.6 or earlier, upgrade before rebuilding. The <a href="/insights/pgvector-087-cve-upgrade">0.8.7 security fix</a> covers IVFFlat builds, and the upgrade is a good moment to schedule the rebuild window.</p>

